data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":2474,"item_id":"fc_01f00a726c27b4d0016adb322e1d948191913e6578693c4fd8","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"BRIDGE_COMPLETED_CANDIDATE_NOT_AUDITED\\\",\\\"bridge_statement\\\":\\\"For a metric space X, a subset A, exponent a>0, and scale δ>0, define C_a(A,δ)=inf_{(U_k)∈Cover_ℕ(A,δ)} Σ'k (ediam U_k)^a in ℝ≥0∞, where Cover_ℕ(A,δ) consists of sequences of subsets U_k with A⊆⋃k U_k and ediam(U_k)≤δ for every k. Empty sets and zero-diameter members are allowed; their costs are zero for a>0. Then μH[a] A = sup_{δ>0} C_a(A,δ) = lim_{δ↓0} C_a(A,δ), with no multiplicative normalization factor. In particular μH[a] A=∞ implies C_a(A,δ)→∞ as δ↓0. For the lower slicing step, if a=s>0 and (V_i) is any countable δ-cover of F=E×[0,1], with ρ_i=ediam(V_i)≤δ, and open intervals J_i⊇π₂(V_i) of lengths at most ρ_i+η_i, then pointwise on [0,1], C_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ'i 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s. The corresponding ENNReal Tonelli argument gives C_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ'i ρ_i^(s+1)+ε whenever Σ'i η_iρ_i^s≤ε. Taking ε↓0 and then the infimum over all such covers gives C_s(E,δ)≤C_{s+1}(F,δ). These statements are source/representation bridge claims, not a proof of the whole product-dimension theorem.\\\",\\\"complete_derivation\\\":\\\"Use the pinned library's extended metric diameter ediam, taking values in ℝ≥0∞, and exponentiation in ℝ≥0∞. For positive real a, identify a with its NNReal/ENNReal coercion as appropriate. Define C_a(A,δ) as the infimum over Nat-indexed covers above; this is the candidate's countable covering content in the exact representation used by the library. A sequence is a countable cover (finite covers are represented by adjoining empty sets). Since a>0, (0)^a=0, so zero-diameter members, including singletons, have zero cost. For ordinary metric spaces, ediam(U) is the NNReal diameter coerced to ENNReal; the coercion commutes with the positive real power, so this definition agrees with the unnormalized sum of ordinary diameters to power a. No conversion from balls or from a different convention is being made.\\\\n\\\\nUnfold the pinned formula Measure.hausdorffMeasure_apply. For each r>0 the inner infimum there is exactly C_a(A,r): it uses Nat-indexed covering sequences, the same cover condition, the same diameter bound, and tsum of ediam(U_k)^a. The extra supremum over witnesses that U_k is nonempty contributes precisely ediam(U_k)^a for nonempty U_k and zero for empty U_k; with a>0 this is also the ordinary term ediam(U_k)^a, since an empty set has diameter zero. Thus μH[a] A = sup_{r>0} C_a(A,r), exactly, not merely up to a positive constant. For δ₁≤δ₂, the δ₁-admissible covers are a subset of the δ₂-admissible covers, hence C_a(A,δ₁)≥C_a(A,δ₂). Therefore the limit as δ decreases to zero exists in [0,∞] and equals sup_{δ>0} C_a(A,δ), by the definition of the monotone one-sided limit (equivalently, positive scales are cofinal at zero). Consequently μH[a]A=∞ implies for every finite M there is δ₀>0 such that 0<δ≤δ₀ implies C_a(A,δ)>M. No finite-scale equality between content and measure is asserted.\\\\n\\\\nFor the slicing integration, discard empty V_i and enumerate the remaining countably many members by a subset of ℕ (or retain the original countable index and use the same proof). Coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz for the concatenated Euclidean metric, so diam(π₁V_i),diam(π₂V_i)≤ρ_i. For each t and x∈E, (x,t) belongs to some V_i; hence the sets π₁(V_i) over indices with t∈π₂(V_i) cover E. Since π₂(V_i)⊆J_i, the π₁(V_i) over indices with t∈J_i also cover E. Their diameters are at most δ. Thus the definition of content and monotonicity of tsum give C_s(E,δ)≤Σ'i 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s for every t∈[0,1]. The right side is a nonnegative measurable ENNReal-valued function: each J_i∩[0,1] is Borel, its indicator is measurable, and multiplication by the constant ρ_i^s preserves measurability. Integrate with the restricted Lebesgue measure on [0,1], whose mass is 1. The pointwise inequality implies the integral inequality by lintegral_mono (or its a.e. form). By lintegral_tsum, applicable because the index type is countable and each summand is measurable, the integral of the series is the tsum of the integrals. Each term's integral is ρ_i^s times the measure of J_i∩[0,1], hence is at most ρ_i^s(ρ_i+η_i); interval length is at most ρ_i+η_i. Therefore C_s(E,δ)≤Σ'i ρ_i^(s+1)+Σ'iη_iρ_i^s≤Σ'iρ_i^(s+1)+ε. For a sequence indexed by positive integers, η_i=ε·2^(−i)/(1+ρ_i^s) is positive and gives Σ'iη_iρ_i^s≤ε; zero-diameter terms contribute zero because s>0. Let ε↓0 to obtain C_s(E,δ)≤Σ'iρ_i^(s+1) for each chosen cover. Taking the infimum over all countable δ-covers yields C_s(E,δ)≤C_{s+1}(F,δ). Combined with the exact small-scale bridge, μH[s]E=∞ implies C_s(E,δ)→∞ and hence C_{s+1}(F,δ)→∞. The same bridge then gives μH[s+1]F=∞. The library dimension theorem can then be applied at NNReal exponent s+1. This repairs the candidate's ordinary-integral phrasing: the direct library-safe formulation is the ENNReal lintegral on the restricted interval, not an unqualified real integral.\\\\n\\\\nExponent bookkeeping: the library Hausdorff measure accepts d:ℝ in hausdorffMeasure_apply, while dimH threshold theorems quantify d:ℝ≥0. For the slicing argument fix real s>0, coerce it to a NNReal d_s, and use the measure at the real coercion of d_s; preserve the coercion in the bridge rather than silently identifying scalar types. Powers at diameter zero vanish because s>0. The candidate's use of arbitrary real exponents is thus valid at this step only after the indicated coercion. This bridge does not cover exponent zero, and does not need to: the candidate's positive-exponent lower-bound branch assumes 0<s<D. Its separate D=0 argument remains unchanged. The n=0 and zero-dimensional E cases are likewise unchanged; this bridge makes no positive-dimension assumption on E, only s>0 for zero-diameter costs.\\\",\\\"source_inventory\\\":[{\\\"source\\\":\\\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean:535-552; especially hausdorffMeasure_apply at 547-552\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"pinned library definition/formula\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"hausdorffMeasure d is mkMetric (fun r => r^d), and its application is a supremum over positive ENNReal scales, then infimum over Nat-indexed covers with ediam≤scale and a tsum of powers; the nonempty-witness supremum convention is explicit.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Inspected source; source candidate, not Audit A or a Lean signature certification.\\\"},{\\\"source\\\":\\\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean:499-519\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"general theorem candidate\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"mkMetric_le_liminf_tsum bounds the metric measure by liminf of costs of countable covers at scales tending to zero; Hausdorff specialization is at 556-560.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Inspected; not needed for the exact supremum/content identity, but available for alternative cover estimates.\\\"},{\\\"source\\\":\\\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean:556-560\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"general theorem candidate\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"hausdorffMeasure_le_liminf_tsum, with countable index types, scale tending to zero, eventual diameter bound and eventual covering hypothesis.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Inspected source; application conditions must be instantiated at a formal use.\\\"},{\\\"source\\\":\\\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Lebesgue/Add.lean:360-367\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"general theorem candidate\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"lintegral_tsum: for countable index and each summand AEMeasurable, lintegral of tsum equals tsum of lintegrals.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Inspected source; slicing application additionally establishes measurability and computes each indicator integral.\\\"},{\\\"source\\\":\\\"Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean:97-99, 109-135\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"definition and threshold theorem candidates\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"dimH takes values in ENNReal; hausdorffMeasure_of_lt_dimH at 114-118 and hausdorffMeasure_of_dimH_lt at 130-135 give the below/above threshold implications for NNReal exponents.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Inspected source; exact typeclass/metric-space instantiation and exponent coercions remain formalization work.\\\"},{\\\"source\\\":\\\"completed-proof.json: lower-bound argument and source_obligations\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"candidate proof input\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The slicing route uses projected covers, open interval enlargement, and a nonnegative indicator series.\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"Preserved; repaired the content bridge and formulated integration as ENNReal lintegral/Tonelli.\\\"}],\\\"remaining_obligations\\\":[\\\"This is a candidate bridge, not fresh Audit A, a structural NL0 check, or Lean certification. A final handoff must probe actual elaborated signatures and typeclass instances, especially the diameter coercions, content infimum representation, measurable indicators, interval-restricted Lebesgue measure, lintegral_mono, and lintegral_tsum.\\\",\\\"The candidate's dimension-threshold citations, Lipschitz projection argument for D=0, finite ambient dimension argument, upper covering argument, and Euclidean metric correspondence are outside this bridge and retain their own source/application obligations.\\\",\\\"A routine correction to the candidate's integration prose is required: state the integral as an ENNReal lintegral over Lebesgue measure restricted to [0,1], explicitly invoke monotonicity and countable Tonelli, then calculate each indicator integral. Do not call an ordinary integral without establishing its meaning and hypotheses.\\\",\\\"The bridge intentionally does not assume the desired product-dimension equality and does not alter the proof's slicing route or its n=0 and D=0 branches.\\\"]}\"}"}

